SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 320 | Thursday, March 26, 2026

Good morning.

1) February trade: imports from Vietnam and Mexico up, China down again
The country detail shows the pattern that has held for two years: China's share of US goods imports fell to 12.8%, Mexico is at 16.1%, and Vietnam passed Japan for the first time. The category detail is more interesting: the shift is concentrated in electronics assembly and furniture. Industrial machinery imports from China actually rose.

2) A federal truck-parking grant program clears committee
$755M over five years for states to build or expand truck parking along interstate corridors. Drivers lose about an hour a day looking for parking, per the industry's own surveys. The bill has bipartisan support and has died in committee twice before; third time is not necessarily different.

3) Class 8 orders bounce to 19,200 in March to date
Still weak against history, but the first month-over-month increase since last summer. Fleets say the emissions-rule clarity, however grudging, was the trigger. Suppliers who furloughed in February are calling people back.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 96%. Occupancy at public refrigerated warehouses in the Fresno-Visalia corridor as of this week, up from 94% a week ago. Stone fruit waits for no one.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

